(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physical-diagnosis-1-exam-3-musculoskeletal-exam-quiz"]={"title":"Musculoskeletal Physical Examination — Set 1 (Physical Diagnosis 1)","category":"Physical Diagnosis","questions":[{"q":"Which fibrous bands connect bone to bone?","choices":["Tendons","Bursae","Cartilage","Ligaments"],"answer":3,"exp":"Ligaments are ropelike bundles of fibrous tissue that connect bone to bone. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 3"},{"q":"Which structures connect muscle to bone?","choices":["Ligaments","Tendons","Bursae","Synovium"],"answer":1,"exp":"Tendons are inelastic strong fibrous collagen fibers that connect muscle to bone. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 3"},{"q":"What is a bursa?","choices":["A band that connects bone to bone","A dense collagen matrix overlying a bony surface","A pouch of synovial fluid that cushions movement over a joint","A fibrous cord attaching muscle to bone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Bursae are pouches of synovial fluid that cushion the movement of tendons and muscles over bone or other joint structures. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 3"},{"q":"Which joint type is freely movable, with bones covered by articular cartilage and separated by a fluid-filled cavity?","choices":["A fibrous joint","A cartilaginous joint","A synovial joint","A juxta-articular joint"],"answer":2,"exp":"A synovial joint is freely movable, its bones covered by articular cartilage and separated by a synovial cavity whose membrane secretes lubricating synovial fluid. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 5"},{"q":"Which type of joint has essentially no appreciable movement?","choices":["A synovial joint","A cartilaginous joint","A spheroidal joint","A fibrous joint"],"answer":3,"exp":"A fibrous joint has no appreciable movement, its bones separated by fibrous tissue or cartilage. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 7"},{"q":"A cartilaginous joint, such as between vertebral bodies, is described as what?","choices":["Slightly movable, with bones separated by fibrocartilaginous discs","Freely movable, with a synovial cavity","Completely immovable, with fused bone","Freely movable, with a true ball-and-socket shape"],"answer":0,"exp":"A cartilaginous joint is slightly movable, with bones separated by fibrocartilaginous discs whose nucleus pulposus cushions bony movement. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 7"},{"q":"Which synovial joint type is a ball-and-socket allowing wide-ranging motion, as in the shoulder and hip?","choices":["Hinge joint","Spheroidal joint","Condylar joint","Fibrous joint"],"answer":1,"exp":"A spheroidal (ball-and-socket) joint fits a convex surface into a concave cavity, allowing wide-ranging flexion, extension, abduction, adduction, rotation, and circumduction, as in the shoulder and hip. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 6"},{"q":"A hinge joint, such as the elbow or interphalangeal joints, permits what motion?","choices":["Wide-ranging motion in every plane","Motion in one plane — flexion and extension","No appreciable movement at all","Only slight gliding movement"],"answer":1,"exp":"A hinge joint permits motion in one plane, flexion and extension, as in the elbow and the interphalangeal joints of the hand and foot. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 6"},{"q":"When taking a musculoskeletal history for an injury, which question addresses the 'mechanism of injury'?","choices":["What is your temperature right now?","What is your blood type?","How did the injury happen?","Who is your primary care provider?"],"answer":2,"exp":"The mechanism of injury asks how the injury happened, a key part of an accurate musculoskeletal history. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which is listed as a common or concerning musculoskeletal symptom?","choices":["Improved energy levels after exercise","Joint pain with fever, chills, rash, or weight loss","A normal appetite with no pain","Full painless range of motion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Joint pain accompanied by systemic features such as fever, chills, rash, anorexia, weight loss, or weakness is a concerning musculoskeletal symptom. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 8"},{"q":"In the musculoskeletal exam, what does the 'IPROMS' order of examination stand for?","choices":["Injection, Percussion, Reflexes, and Motor strength","Inspection, Palpation, Range of motion, Special maneuvers","Inspection, Percussion, Reflexes, Sensation","Imaging, Palpation, Reduction, Splinting"],"answer":1,"exp":"The IPROMS order of the musculoskeletal exam is Inspection, Palpation, Range of Motion, and Special maneuvers. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 10"},{"q":"During inspection of the musculoskeletal system, the examiner primarily does what?","choices":["Uses the eyes to observe posture, symmetry, and deformities","Uses the hands to feel for tenderness, warmth, and swelling","Moves each joint through its range of motion","Strikes tendons to elicit reflexes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Inspection means using the eyes to observe the patient's general posture, body symmetry, and any apparent deformities, curvature, muscle wasting, or swelling. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 12"},{"q":"During palpation of a joint, the examiner assesses for what?","choices":["Visual curvature of the spine","Reflex responses to a hammer","Tenderness, warmth, and irregularities","The patient's active range of motion"],"answer":2,"exp":"Palpation uses the hands to feel the joints, muscles, and surrounding tissues for tenderness, warmth, or irregularities. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 13"},{"q":"What distinguishes active range of motion (active ROM) from passive range of motion?","choices":["In active ROM the examiner moves the joint for the patient","In active ROM the joint is imaged rather than moved","In active ROM the patient performs the movement themselves","In active ROM the joint is held completely still"],"answer":2,"exp":"In active range of motion, the patient actively performs the movement, whereas in passive range of motion the examiner moves the joint. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 15"},{"q":"During passive range of motion, what does the examiner do?","choices":["The examiner moves the patient's joint through its range","The patient moves the joint entirely on their own","The joint is struck to elicit a reflex","The joint is only observed, not moved"],"answer":0,"exp":"In passive range of motion the examiner passively moves the patient's joint to assess range of motion, comparing the end points to the active range. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 16"},{"q":"On the 0-to-5 muscle strength scale, what does a grade of 5 indicate?","choices":["Full range of motion against gravity with full resistance","Full range of motion, but not against any gravity","Only a trace of movement","No evidence of movement at all"],"answer":0,"exp":"A muscle strength grade of 5 is full range of motion against gravity with full resistance — normal strength. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 18"},{"q":"On the muscle strength scale, a grade of 3 corresponds to what?","choices":["No evidence of any muscle movement","Full range of motion against gravity but not against resistance","Full muscle strength against complete applied resistance","Movement only with gravity eliminated"],"answer":1,"exp":"Grade 3 muscle strength is full range of motion against gravity but not against added resistance. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 18"},{"q":"A muscle that shows only a trace of movement is graded as what?","choices":["Grade 1","Grade 0","Grade 3","Grade 5"],"answer":0,"exp":"A trace of muscle movement is graded 1 on the 0-to-5 muscle strength scale. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 18"},{"q":"To palpate the temporomandibular joint, where does the examiner place the fingertips?","choices":["Just in front of the tragus of each ear","Over the mastoid process behind each ear","At the angle of the mandible near the chin","Along the midline of the hard palate"],"answer":0,"exp":"To assess the temporomandibular joint, the examiner places the fingertips just in front of the tragus of each ear and has the patient open and close the mouth. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 20"},{"q":"Which movement is tested as part of temporomandibular joint range of motion?","choices":["Shrugging the shoulders upward","Rotating the neck to look over the shoulder","Flexing the elbow to ninety degrees","Moving the jaw laterally to each side"],"answer":3,"exp":"Temporomandibular joint range of motion includes opening and closing the mouth, moving the jaw laterally to each side, and protruding and retracting the jaw. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 20"},{"q":"Which cervical range-of-motion maneuver is normally about 70 degrees and occurs mainly at the C1-C2 level?","choices":["Forward flexion — chin to chest","Rotation — looking over each shoulder","Hyperextension — looking at the ceiling","Lateral bending — ear to shoulder"],"answer":1,"exp":"Cervical rotation, looking over each shoulder, is normally about 70 degrees and occurs primarily at the C1-C2 (atlas-axis) level. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 24"},{"q":"Cervical forward flexion, bringing the chin to the chest, is normally about how many degrees?","choices":["About 90 degrees","About 110 degrees","About 45 degrees","About 160 degrees"],"answer":2,"exp":"Cervical forward flexion, bringing the chin to the chest, is normally about 45 degrees and occurs at the skull and C1 (atlas). — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 24"},{"q":"On inspection of the posterior neck, the spinous process is most prominent at which level?","choices":["The C1 and C2 level","The mid-thoracic T6 level","The lumbosacral L5 level","The C7 and T1 level"],"answer":3,"exp":"The spinous process is most prominent at the C7 and T1 level and serves as a surface landmark when inspecting the posterior spine. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 22"},{"q":"A lateral curvature of the thoracic and lumbar spine, assessed by inspecting from behind, is called what?","choices":["Scoliosis","Lordosis","Crepitus","Bursitis"],"answer":0,"exp":"A lateral curvature of the spine, assessed by inspecting the thoracic and lumbar spine from behind, is called scoliosis. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which maneuver tests forward flexion of the thoracic and lumbar spine (normally about 40 to 60 degrees)?","choices":["Asking the patient to shrug the shoulders","Asking the patient to look up at the ceiling","Asking the patient to make a fist","Asking the patient to try to touch their toes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Thoracolumbar forward flexion, normally about 40 to 60 degrees, is tested by asking the patient to try to touch their toes. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 30"},{"q":"The glenohumeral joint of the shoulder is formed by which two structures?","choices":["The medial clavicle and the upper sternum","The lateral clavicle and the acromion","The head of the humerus and the glenoid fossa of the scapula","The radial head and the capitulum of the humerus"],"answer":2,"exp":"The glenohumeral joint is formed by the head of the humerus articulating with the glenoid fossa of the scapula. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 32"},{"q":"The acromioclavicular joint of the shoulder is formed by which structures?","choices":["The lateral end of the clavicle with the acromion of the scapula","The head of the humerus with the glenoid fossa of the scapula","The medial clavicle with the upper sternum","The scapula with the posterior rib cage"],"answer":0,"exp":"The acromioclavicular joint is formed by the lateral end of the clavicle articulating with the acromion of the scapula. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 32"},{"q":"The rotator cuff muscles are remembered by the mnemonic 'SITS.' Which muscles does it represent?","choices":["Sternocleidomastoid, iliopsoas, trapezius, soleus","Supraspinatus, infraspinatus, teres minor, subscapularis","Supinator, interossei, triceps, sartorius","Subclavius, intercostals, teres major, serratus"],"answer":1,"exp":"The rotator cuff mnemonic SITS stands for the supraspinatus, infraspinatus, teres minor, and subscapularis. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 32"},{"q":"Normal elbow flexion, bending the elbow, is approximately how many degrees?","choices":["About 45 degrees","About 90 degrees","About 220 degrees","About 160 degrees"],"answer":3,"exp":"Normal elbow flexion, bending the elbow, is approximately 160 degrees, with extension at about 0 to negative 5 degrees. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 41"},{"q":"Which additional detail is part of an accurate musculoskeletal pain history?","choices":["The patient's favorite hobby","The color of the examination room","The examiner's own glove size","The duration of the complaint"],"answer":3,"exp":"An accurate musculoskeletal history includes the mechanism of injury, the location and nature of pain, a pain scale, the duration of the complaint, and exacerbating or relieving maneuvers. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 8"}]};
